      how_t: "How does it work?", how_s: "From space to your phone, in four steps.",
      h1_t: "Satellites spot the fires", h1_d: "Five NASA and NOAA satellites fly over the Sahel several times a day and have spotted every hot spot on the ground since 2000.",
      h2_t: "FireSync makes 25 years comparable", h2_d: "Newer satellites see smaller fires than older ones. FireSync corrects this gap so each season can be compared with the previous ones.",
      h3_t: "We test it on the past", h3_d: "Every method is tested on seasons it has never seen, to measure its reliability honestly.",
      h4_t: "The AI forecasts and alerts", h4_d: "Every week: a 4-week risk map, a \"where to act\" ranking, an SMS / WhatsApp message and PDF bulletins.",
      faq_t: "Key words, explained simply", faq_s: "No need to be a specialist: here is what the numbers shown by FireSync mean.",
      faq: [
        ["What is a fire \"detection\"?", "When a satellite passes over a fire, it spots a very hot point on the ground: that is a detection. A small fire gives one detection, a large fire can give several. FireSync counts these detections, all converted to the eye of the same satellite (VIIRS) so that years can be compared."],
        ["What does \"normal\" mean?", "It is what is usually observed at the same time of year, computed over the last 25 seasons (the middle value, called the median). A season \"within normal\" looks like most past years."],
        ["And the \"percentile\"?", "It ranks the value against the past. Percentile 80: there were more fires than in 80% of past seasons at the same date. Between 25 and 75 is normal; above 90 is exceptional."],
        ["How are the risk levels set?", "High: clearly more fires than usual, and in large numbers. Moderate: more fires than usual. Normal or below: nothing unusual. Low activity: fewer than 10 fires expected."],
        ["What does the range of a forecast mean?", "A forecast gives a likely value and a range. In 9 cases out of 10, the real number of fires falls within that range: the wider it is, the greater the uncertainty."],
        ["What does \"×1.3 vs normal\" mean?", "That 1.3 times as many fires are expected as in an ordinary year at the same time, i.e. 30% more. \"×0.8\" means 20% fewer."],
        ["Why \"harmonise\" the satellites?", "Newer satellites (VIIRS, since 2012) see smaller fires than older ones (MODIS, since 2000). Without correction, fires would seem to triple in 2012. FireSync converts the older observations so that 25 years of data can be compared."],
        ["Can the forecasts be trusted?", "They were tested on 12 past seasons: they beat the simple average of past years and their probabilities are reliable. But they do not yet know the weather: use them as an aid, together with field experience."],
        ["Is it free?", "Yes. NASA data are open and the platform is free. A pro space (free, on request) lets you follow your own areas and receive alerts."],
      ],
